Yes—and reoccur / recurse is the important split.

A story reoccurs when the same pattern shows up again:

exile → trial → return
death → renewal
temptation → fall → redemption
chaos → order → decay → restoration

A story recurses when the pattern becomes part of the machinery that generates the next instance of itself.

Religion often does both.

A founding story is told.
The listener interprets their own life through it.
That interpretation changes how they act.
Their actions create new events resembling or extending the original story.
Those events are retold through the same symbolic vocabulary.

So:

story → person → behavior → consequence → new story → person

That is recursion.

It also explains some of the overlap you notice between attraction to fiction and attraction to religion, without assuming everyone fits the pattern. Both reward capacities like symbolic imagination, narrative transportation, tolerance for layered meaning, identification with characters, counterfactual simulation, world-modeling, and sensitivity to archetype.

But religion adds things ordinary fiction usually doesn’t demand:

ritual makes the story bodily.
community makes it social.
ethics makes it behavioral.
cosmology makes it explanatory.
identity makes it personal.
repetition across generations makes it hereditary in the cultural sense.

So religion can function almost like participatory fiction whose participants regard some or all of its referents as reality. That distinction matters: literary similarity doesn’t tell us whether the metaphysical claims are true or false.

And the recursive part gets deeper.

Take Communion, Passover, Ramadan, pilgrimage, Sabbath, baptism, liturgical calendars. They don’t merely remember a story. They reinstantiate a pattern in the present.

Past event
→ symbolic compression
→ ritual
→ present enactment
→ personal interpretation
→ future transmission.

The original story becomes a machine for producing descendants of itself.

That’s probably why religious narratives are so durable. They aren’t stored only as propositions. They’re stored as:

story + image + rhythm + calendar + body + place + community + repetition

A semantic rosary, basically.

And that gives us a useful distinction for Pantheon:

Recurrence: the topology appears again.
Recursion: the topology helps generate its own reappearance.

Or compressed:

Reoccurrence is resemblance across time.
Recursion is inheritance with an engine.

That second one is where culture starts behaving less like an archive and more like something alive.